#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3d5ffe46-c0ac-5309-a31b-28a6a1d927e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9054" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ce1e5bc9489f54f405f96a74e4e02458149be011a952f91603d6cd22e9b0a01.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/07/07d28376d40c024d22c60085ccac3f600a057e45e624e522c815755aaf8fe8e6.svg)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/6d/6dcb4edb0b165ab337b54783c4f30015379224e1ee156876b459666cc760de81.svg)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/42/42b6edeb0a13e90007c6c6f15a68440c3b7a00727654d6db0ce69f5b2bd65913.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/7c/7c4372cfe047a9f2ddaf90e15750975da6d562f5287aba2bfe414335dba56b42.svg)\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "30daf182-0ed6-5ddd-994e-a5af7c6c6c01" :question/math-academy-id "e-9056" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-5I$ on the nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w},\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v}_n$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}2$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank ${\\color{red}2},$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+{\\color{blue}3}I$ maps $\\mathbf{u}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}3$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}3$   corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-3}$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} \\xrightarrow{A-{\\color{black}5}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-{\\color{black}5}I$ maps $\\mathbf{y}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}1$ step, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}1,$ not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "79ffef9f-8be0-581f-b2f1-dc796d2cf94b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9057" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a494e86e18d97ca9332738cc6d85582a71b6933bb6afe6621b83e675c2272fb9.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\n- First, we compute $A-I{:}$\n\n![](images/ef/efe92eb57e67b1113330fecc7009cf59d3a06f0f5f33ac8e800804f0067ff9eb.svg)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/3c/3ca24127a41102d7b4f5f7118620ccc88c3452cc10dfd68a2f38c4b767435342.svg)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/c9/c92357786ae2b400582794c004d4c0c582fa07fbd8822a8dbc1a871d953b72b2.svg)\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/91/91fd2e0b4a21407faf575572ed915fc546b85edb03a7077417425bd1c2ee98e7.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/8b/8b6b7659b3c647858a709e6ae1b1ce482ab940bba9b2d01280da4bbe47c12fdd.svg)\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b9c8ea-3433-5e98-ae2b-66d5074675b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362656} {:answer/id #uuid "d30126e1-6458-5988-a441-53d4d29bf115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362657} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9f5762-5d97-545d-8ca2-b371d5cdf90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362658} {:answer/id #uuid "16e7f43b-5245-5b84-8f13-c4e0178a0177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362659} {:answer/id #uuid "b4447d56-1641-5e1f-ac40-5127d0aecf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b9c8ea-3433-5e98-ae2b-66d5074675b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362656} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9c9380-d4e5-5d77-bc33-cb4ca372f9b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7971d731-7317-51d2-bfe1-46fc4b23c3f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109117" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+3I:$\n\n$$\nA+3I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22442fa5-3ac2-5b6d-8550-6ad60d10e199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241477} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb021b2-1015-50c9-82f0-4e3db31fe829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241864} {:answer/id #uuid "664f1704-9bf7-5ed2-bdcd-e673e693ceac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242104} {:answer/id #uuid "67b18cc0-15fc-5a64-b685-24c74f1d28a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242111} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a32f11-c203-592f-95cc-5eba4cbdfb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "664f1704-9bf7-5ed2-bdcd-e673e693ceac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242104} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b988a7-f80c-506d-a67f-2ddc646b8c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cf2c42d-88e3-50f2-bb3a-16ab087d4389" :question/math-academy-id "q-109236" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nII. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I=A-1I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b612074-81a0-5d6f-aec5-96eeb7af6bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433691} {:answer/id #uuid "5c580e8c-17f9-5329-b05e-162735ed92b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433692} {:answer/id #uuid "71851cec-299e-55b4-b56b-b60fb7b9e7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433697} {:answer/id #uuid "85817bc0-a638-5f2e-8f8a-cdeeff6b3d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433699} {:answer/id #uuid "c9aab259-7b9a-54be-b6ec-554fd1c11b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85817bc0-a638-5f2e-8f8a-cdeeff6b3d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433699} :answer-field/id #uuid "59068b8c-c566-5e5f-a6b7-89faa773c3aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c32d62b6-73f0-5c6b-b445-e1066fb0b96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109242" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+2I$ and $A-2I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector of $A$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d2b9d26-8293-5a06-81bf-a5d8768ab1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241725} {:answer/id #uuid "4e059d3f-50a9-5d66-bbba-0d70399855ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241881} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd4d0aa-ccaf-528d-a95d-b920e6aa13a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242177} {:answer/id #uuid "76f397bc-53a2-5ba3-b32c-891ac2f4b5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242234} {:answer/id #uuid "9de02649-0cd7-593b-a2c5-9ca8945e60f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fd4d0aa-ccaf-528d-a95d-b920e6aa13a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242177} :answer-field/id #uuid "417546af-5e04-5c33-856e-84ad4da01a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a61bdaa-5171-5017-8ae8-0bac73d29cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109244" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-6I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e9efe6-968d-54c9-ba7f-d8bfc0344af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241358} {:answer/id #uuid "20dac993-1771-57da-aea4-5a53bae88d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241464} {:answer/id #uuid "971f6555-302a-5456-818a-0917a567ea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242505} {:answer/id #uuid "b7db1f32-24e0-5572-949c-0c739c96c41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242791} {:answer/id #uuid "d20d30f0-c9d8-57d3-8218-55ec4117f2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20dac993-1771-57da-aea4-5a53bae88d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241464} :answer-field/id #uuid "04917658-3d46-5d2e-85f2-f45f68c5a185" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65e6b2ee-a472-5db0-bf96-384d33845a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109360" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+9I$ and $A-I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-9$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $z$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n and $v\\ne 0,$ \ni.e. $A+9I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is  false. Since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, since \n\n$$\nz\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-1I$ maps $z$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3528dc7a-edc0-54b3-bc5e-f088015cc468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241644} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8d0b0d-10a8-5050-9ccf-f84b7ae89c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241701} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc42dce-cb33-5ebd-81ab-a940ad3e8eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242283} {:answer/id #uuid "9efcde9e-50cc-58ad-9453-2e8c5b64cabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242575} {:answer/id #uuid "a430d0ea-44d9-550f-8830-1b2b2a892158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3528dc7a-edc0-54b3-bc5e-f088015cc468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241644} :answer-field/id #uuid "16edc7c5-1547-5425-97d2-8194abf4981c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dab8a46d-1b84-5b26-b9d0-b9d19dd8bea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109440" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+4I$ and $A+3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23feb840-9ea0-5b4a-9981-47d41adc532e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433687} {:answer/id #uuid "31251623-175e-5172-b53d-93dabfd51773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433688} {:answer/id #uuid "e31b4d52-32a8-5673-be5d-c721da9231aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433706} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc1826a-9a0c-57f0-84eb-fb71a37f6300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433707} {:answer/id #uuid "ef393691-7df5-5626-987f-128f055c264d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebc1826a-9a0c-57f0-84eb-fb71a37f6300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433707} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5f1e17-ee64-5f88-acce-1fb89abad6f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3d67809-78b0-59dc-9828-ea082eca69bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109476" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4123c97a-32f0-5090-ad8f-278e9a0415cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433689} {:answer/id #uuid "58792cac-2b0d-543d-8e9e-c8057305744f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433690} {:answer/id #uuid "72e1debb-2d7b-595f-85d2-469a31d07a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433698} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fda644-47ed-58ee-9a8b-8a80fc0d503b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433702} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4e6251-9cc8-5873-b9c2-aab8505ef2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e1debb-2d7b-595f-85d2-469a31d07a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433698} :answer-field/id #uuid "148bd3ea-4973-557b-89b5-48219c1a378a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab77c5c3-a1c5-5710-a08d-82e66f9fdecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109488" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d3a269-f7df-5be8-8c0a-c58771bf3313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241676} {:answer/id #uuid "869c2a77-c110-5a9e-8730-019b98ad1d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242360} {:answer/id #uuid "9e265f89-9d57-5ba5-951b-b7eabd9027cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242565} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5e9232-a129-548e-b486-ec287dd87153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242988} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a3256d-e444-52f1-9bf1-ed1666c753df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a3256d-e444-52f1-9bf1-ed1666c753df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243361} :answer-field/id #uuid "f900ef13-9248-52f7-8b67-b6ec2ce93fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f8f24d7-923f-567b-b949-576aeecc58aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-109506" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine out statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-3I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131723b1-04e0-5afb-b47f-b4d05d1df63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244490} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd2eb1f-1d6e-59fb-9827-b17359aaf4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245131} {:answer/id #uuid "77bdf862-1837-5e8b-a68a-3e5fa2a3385d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245375} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2ca2da-7622-54e5-a685-fcc2f8d40a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245841} {:answer/id #uuid "aee5383f-bf90-5f85-aadc-05f837be2ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aee5383f-bf90-5f85-aadc-05f837be2ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245873} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dae667a-3a97-5762-926d-1ea73a981d60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c9bad97-e078-5fd9-8cb8-6324032501fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109508" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+4I:$\n\n$$\nA+4I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16957b53-7f2c-5897-8254-366068b7d73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433686} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc5b2e1-1001-57bc-9ebf-e1899b27c976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433693} {:answer/id #uuid "698b7655-84cd-57ff-b48c-076442522213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433695} {:answer/id #uuid "974e648d-43b7-5056-bead-1b5c05bf7686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433700} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cec6f6-74c4-595e-b9a4-e8d9615d9af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1cec6f6-74c4-595e-b9a4-e8d9615d9af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433704} :answer-field/id #uuid "263fbd38-e795-53f6-b66f-cb287c466369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93f4dbff-c62f-571f-9c49-e5503854925a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109518" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0898420b-4c54-5e1d-97e3-5cc4bfdd8109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433685} {:answer/id #uuid "68d2143e-ca23-5a4c-95d7-62217b46497f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433694} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0f6d67-6d78-567d-a365-5c0702b884e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433696} {:answer/id #uuid "adc06e2a-797f-5b5b-b125-a1b83e296a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433701} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcccae4-29dd-5752-98f9-f75c35190636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbcccae4-29dd-5752-98f9-f75c35190636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433705} :answer-field/id #uuid "287f4509-af62-597e-a469-54e8dc29ab13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72a34fdf-b3e5-500c-96f3-76e3e66d1191" :question/math-academy-id "q-109572" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362684} {:answer/id #uuid "fea449ab-857f-5e97-a2dd-9a5decfbfe3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362685} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e9c614-fb32-5fc3-a8e1-80768c7a6164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362686} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8fd250-9f94-565b-afb8-d6406e6e4d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362687} {:answer/id #uuid "e4559c11-53ee-582b-ac61-017f9bf594ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362684} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc073027-8d63-5f72-bb4e-ddd774541c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c9089c-246d-51fb-b493-4538acc22376" :question/math-academy-id "q-109574" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18659916-aaf4-573c-9a3b-15946350b712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860