tor corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c26e67-c3dc-5457-b6e3-77c851c0563e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244395} {:answer/id #uuid "86ea91a4-0172-570f-a092-67a3172cc408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245501} {:answer/id #uuid "8c01cea2-3a2e-55ed-934c-7393ce2b8c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245541} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8608d9-2139-58da-9594-cdc1a336f36f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245724} {:answer/id #uuid "a41e13bf-5794-5d2c-bb9c-a67f9ff83aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41e13bf-5794-5d2c-bb9c-a67f9ff83aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245779} :answer-field/id #uuid "82ade937-dfe0-5ac2-9cc4-812779563b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f4f929d-c8e6-554e-bc37-364f30077a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109587" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+5I:$\n\n$$\nA+5I=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
